WEBVTT

00:00:39.400 --> 00:00:50.500
From another world, a smell detector, investigating the path ahead.

00:00:53.430 --> 00:00:55.820
We don't often see a snail that way.

00:00:57.270 --> 00:00:59.900
And that's because we've only recently had...

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:05.660
The tiny lenses and electronic cameras that we need to explore this miniature world.

00:01:10.420 --> 00:01:13.220
But when we meet its inhabitants face to face,

00:01:13.220 --> 00:01:18.260
we suddenly realise that their behaviour can be just as meaningful to us

00:01:18.260 --> 00:01:21.780
as the behaviour of many animals more our own size.

00:01:24.370 --> 00:01:27.940
This, for example. It's an earwig.

00:01:28.350 --> 00:01:28.690
Yes.

00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:32.940
But it's also a female and a mother.

00:01:33.380 --> 00:01:36.740
And like so many mothers, she's guarding her young.

00:01:41.300 --> 00:01:44.760
These two ants are not quite sure whether they like one another.

00:01:45.520 --> 00:01:50.540
Stroking antennae is the equivalent of a cautious chat over the garden fence.

00:01:53.220 --> 00:01:55.900
When big animals go courting, they show off.

00:01:56.020 --> 00:01:58.110
And so do damselflies.

00:02:02.810 --> 00:02:06.810
Courtship signals for a male wolf spider are rather more frantic

00:02:06.810 --> 00:02:12.780
because if his female doesn't understand why he's approaching her, she'll eat him.

00:02:15.430 --> 00:02:19.520
This ant is a farmer, and these aphids, the cows,

00:02:19.820 --> 00:02:22.940
which it milks for a drink of honeydew every day.

00:02:24.430 --> 00:02:28.600
Other ants are eternally on the march.

00:02:30.760 --> 00:02:34.460
Powerfully armed soldiers guard the flanks of their column as they travel,

00:02:34.700 --> 00:02:37.980
protecting the workers who are carrying their helpless young.

00:02:38.200 --> 00:02:48.940
When it comes to craftsmanship, few can beat this wasp using mud

00:02:48.940 --> 00:02:52.910
to construct an elegant jar in which to store her eggs.

00:02:54.920 --> 00:02:57.140
Mud is also used by termites.

00:02:57.630 --> 00:02:59.970
They build tower blocks that in proportion...

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:07.220
to their size are taller than New York skyscrapers. These two worlds, ours and theirs, influence

00:03:07.220 --> 00:03:13.610
one another to an extraordinary degree. If we and the rest of the backbone animals were

00:03:13.610 --> 00:03:20.260
to disappear overnight, the rest of the world would get on pretty well. But if they were

00:03:20.260 --> 00:03:28.640
to disappear, the land's ecosystems would collapse. For the fact is, they were the pioneers.

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:33.880
animals of any kind to colonize the lands of the earth.

00:03:35.460 --> 00:03:38.610
To tell their story, we must go back to a time

00:03:38.930 --> 00:03:41.430
when the world was a very different place.

00:04:00.060 --> 00:04:07.100
400 million years ago, the lands of planet Earth were totally without life.

00:04:07.520 --> 00:04:17.100
They were bare, naked rock, roasted by sun during the day, freezing cold at night, and swept by terrible storms.

00:04:18.040 --> 00:04:22.650
But in the waters of the world, conditions were much more stable.

00:04:24.500 --> 00:04:28.420
Life had begun there some 2,000 million years earlier still.

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:36.660
time it remained microscopic. But eventually, larger animals appeared. Jellyfish and corals,

00:04:36.960 --> 00:04:47.650
starfish and snails, and animals with segmented bodies. All needed food. Many would have eaten

00:04:47.650 --> 00:04:54.010
unguarded eggs, given the chance. And then, around 400 million years ago,

00:04:54.470 --> 00:04:59.430
some enterprising creatures found it safer to lay their eggs out of the sea.

00:05:02.660 --> 00:05:25.050
Every spring, on a few special nights along the Atlantic coast of North America, thousands of horseshoe crabs emerge from the sea.

00:05:32.010 --> 00:05:35.100
And here, in the wet sand, they spawn.

00:05:35.790 --> 00:05:38.900
They may only stay for a few minutes or hours,

00:05:39.060 --> 00:05:42.910
but animals like these may well have been the first of any kind

00:05:42.910 --> 00:05:45.890
to leave the sea and venture onto land.

00:06:03.520 --> 00:06:07.140
Although these creatures spend virtually all their lives at sea,

00:06:07.780 --> 00:06:11.090
they can survive surprisingly well on land.

00:06:11.230 --> 00:06:13.840
It's almost as if they were pre-adapted.

00:06:14.260 --> 00:06:17.130
They have shells, external skeletons,

00:06:17.310 --> 00:06:22.240
and that means that their legs are rigid and jointed.

00:06:22.670 --> 00:06:28.140
And at the back, they have a series of plates called book lungs,

00:06:28.540 --> 00:06:29.680
which extract.

00:06:30.060 --> 00:06:36.010
oxygen from seawater, but can also do the same thing if they're kept reasonably moist

00:06:36.010 --> 00:06:43.800
from the air. So, creatures like this can, in fact, spend about a week on land, and it

00:06:43.800 --> 00:06:49.850
only requires minimal modifications to enable them to live up there permanently.

00:06:54.520 --> 00:06:59.980
It was difficult to abandon the sea altogether until the land became green, but...

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:01.420
Eventually, it did.

00:07:02.060 --> 00:07:05.560
Simple plants, algae, and then mosses and liverworts

00:07:05.560 --> 00:07:09.510
began to advance over the mud and rock to clothe the earth.

00:07:10.210 --> 00:07:15.080
And into these first green tangles came animals looking for food.

00:07:28.450 --> 00:07:29.520
Some had armor.

00:07:30.030 --> 00:07:32.970
For that in the sea had protected them from their enemies.

00:07:33.700 --> 00:07:36.640
Now it would help them conserve moisture.

00:07:52.250 --> 00:07:55.360
They were the ancestors of today's millipedes.

00:08:00.280 --> 00:08:03.480
Small holes had developed along the underside of their bodies

00:08:03.480 --> 00:08:08.720
that led to internal tubes with which they could absorb oxygen from the air.

00:08:13.030 --> 00:08:15.990
Their rigid, jointed legs, however, were largely unchanged

00:08:15.990 --> 00:08:20.010
and worked very well on them, even without the support of water.

00:08:27.160 --> 00:08:29.980
The ring ram heads enabled them to bulldoze their way...

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:43.830
of the vegetation to collect the rotting plants on which they fed. They grew big, increasing the

00:08:43.830 --> 00:08:53.710
number of segments in their bodies. Some had over 300, each with two pairs of legs. Some

00:08:53.830 --> 00:08:58.680
that didn't curl up reinforced their armour with plates along their backs.

00:09:11.200 --> 00:09:13.740
Crustaceans, like shrimps, came too.

00:09:16.660 --> 00:09:19.260
They were the ancestors of wood lice.

00:09:32.780 --> 00:09:37.800
So today, there is a huge and varied population of animals living on the land

00:09:37.800 --> 00:09:43.680
with bodies that are little different from those of their ancestors who lived in the sea so long ago.

00:09:47.400 --> 00:09:50.060
And they are extraordinarily successful.

00:09:50.600 --> 00:09:56.320
Some are the most numerous of all land-living species, but we seldom see them.

00:09:57.120 --> 00:09:59.850
This pin will give you an idea of why.

00:10:02.720 --> 00:10:03.320
They're tiny.

00:10:04.160 --> 00:10:07.420
This minute little creature is a springtail.

00:10:07.930 --> 00:10:12.180
It's less than half a millimetre long, the size of a full stop.

00:10:14.380 --> 00:10:18.600
In one square metre of soil, there may be over 10,000 of them.

00:10:21.060 --> 00:10:23.380
Drying out is a very real danger for them,

00:10:23.520 --> 00:10:29.280
and some waterproof themselves regularly with a droplet of special grooming fluid.

00:10:31.340 --> 00:10:36.000
You might even say that they have turned bathing into an art form.

00:10:42.110 --> 00:10:48.470
They even have two inflatable tubes that enable them to get to those hard-to-reach places.

00:10:52.100 --> 00:10:59.780
To help them get around through the leaf litter, these springtails, as their name suggests, have a rather novel way of jumping.

00:11:07.230 --> 00:11:10.770
They have a tiny two-pronged lever beneath their abdomen.

00:11:11.570 --> 00:11:17.280
One small flick from it can catapult them six inches, some 15 centimetres, into the air.

00:11:23.360 --> 00:11:27.240
It's the equivalent of a human being jumping over the Eiffel Tower.

00:11:30.250 --> 00:11:38.100
And if they happen to land upside down, well, they have a special way of righting themselves.

00:11:38.920 --> 00:11:45.620
They use their grooming fluid dispenser to stick onto the ground so that they can pull themselves back onto their feet.

00:11:53.410 --> 00:11:59.880
So the foundations were laid for the ecosystems that now flourish on Earth and on which.

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:01.160
We ourselves depend.

00:12:05.900 --> 00:12:09.500
It has to be said, however, that sometimes some of us

00:12:09.500 --> 00:12:14.490
regard a few of these pioneers more as our enemies than our friends.

00:12:19.260 --> 00:12:23.350
Many of the mollusks in the sea develop shells

00:12:23.350 --> 00:12:25.550
to protect themselves from predators.

00:12:25.950 --> 00:12:29.970
But on land, those shells serve just as well to keep...

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:36.020
the occupant nice and moist. So without any major change to their anatomy,

00:12:36.420 --> 00:12:41.420
mollusks were able to creep up out of the water and graze in the forests of algae

00:12:41.420 --> 00:12:46.340
and mosses that were then spreading over the land. And given the right conditions,

00:12:46.800 --> 00:12:47.740
they still do.

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:01.760
rain and the coming of night

00:13:01.760 --> 00:13:04.590
a secret army comes out of

00:13:04.590 --> 00:13:13.840
hiding. These are the conditions

00:13:13.840 --> 00:13:16.160
they like best. Dark

00:13:16.160 --> 00:13:18.520
and best of all, wet.

00:13:38.960 --> 00:13:44.620
Gliding along a carpet of slime works just as well on land as it does underwater.

00:13:46.610 --> 00:13:51.030
And a rasping tongue scrapes algae off rocks wherever they are.

00:13:57.150 --> 00:13:59.970
In times of drought, snails may be unable to...

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:01.720
to move around for months on end.

00:14:02.230 --> 00:14:04.050
So when conditions are right,

00:14:04.300 --> 00:14:06.970
they eagerly set off to find food.

00:14:11.560 --> 00:14:14.100
Their upper pair of tentacles carry those eyes

00:14:14.100 --> 00:14:15.500
with which they look around.

00:14:17.330 --> 00:14:19.730
The lower ones smell what's beneath.

00:14:30.510 --> 00:14:34.950
They breathe by means of a small pouch on the right-hand side of their body,

00:14:35.190 --> 00:14:40.490
just within the shell, which, because it's permanently moist, is able to absorb oxygen.

00:14:49.060 --> 00:14:52.560
This is what they're seeking, succulent green leaf.

00:14:53.140 --> 00:14:54.240
No time to be lost.

00:15:05.080 --> 00:15:15.810
Dawn will bring a change in conditions, so they have to return to their shelters and clamp down their shells once more so that they retain their moisture.

00:15:23.400 --> 00:15:29.960
The ancient forests were colonized by all kinds of plant eaters long before there were any frogs or lizards.

00:15:30.000 --> 00:15:37.270
birds, or insect-eating mammals, but there were nonetheless hunters prowling through the

00:15:37.270 --> 00:15:47.800
vegetation. This was one of the first. Fossils very like it have been found in rocks that are 540

00:15:47.800 --> 00:15:56.590
million years old. This is a velvet worm. It too has scarcely changed since it lived in the sea,

00:15:56.930 --> 00:15:59.870
and today it's only found in wet, humid.

00:16:00.030 --> 00:16:06.860
It usually hunts at night, but infrared cameras can reveal it in action.

00:16:09.340 --> 00:16:16.320
Soft, slumpy legs enable it to move in total silence, and it finds its way with long, sensitive feelers.

00:16:17.760 --> 00:16:19.520
It's a master of stealth.

00:16:22.170 --> 00:16:26.510
This cricket has huge eyes, but it's difficult to see what's going on around it.

00:16:31.750 --> 00:16:40.070
Though the velvet worm has fangs, it will attack its prey when it finds it with a very special weapon.

00:17:05.920 --> 00:17:10.870
Anxiously, the cricket probes around in the darkness with its long antennae.

00:17:15.720 --> 00:17:19.540
The velvet worm will only know if it's found its prey when it touches it,

00:17:20.160 --> 00:17:22.480
so when it does, it has to react immediately.

00:17:32.840 --> 00:17:34.420
And the cricket is trapped.

00:17:36.400 --> 00:17:41.790
A slow-motion camera shows the remarkable way in which the velvet worm attacks.

00:17:42.280 --> 00:17:46.760
Two nozzles beneath its feelers squirt twin streams of glue.

00:17:47.080 --> 00:17:59.040
The more the cricket struggles, the more it becomes entangled.

00:18:03.100 --> 00:18:08.340
With the prey immobilized, the velvet worm reclaims its glue by eating it,

00:18:08.580 --> 00:18:10.540
and then it starts on the cricket.

00:18:21.050 --> 00:18:24.090
There were other hunters, too, in the ancient forests,

00:18:24.510 --> 00:18:28.670
relatives of the horseshoe crabs, and they were even more formidable.

00:18:35.240 --> 00:18:37.240
This is a whip spider.

00:18:38.070 --> 00:18:41.560
Like its ancestors, it has a hard external skeleton.

00:18:44.390 --> 00:18:49.410
Two of its limbs have been turned into highly mobile, sensitive feelers.

00:18:49.890 --> 00:18:53.890
It uses them to probe around delicately, both in front and behind.

00:18:54.700 --> 00:18:58.020
Any prey within a foot of it will be immediately detected.

00:19:04.120 --> 00:19:09.200
It's extremely territorial, and it has no hesitation in attacking one of its own kind.

00:19:14.990 --> 00:19:20.820
The width of its claws is a good indicator of strength, and a smaller animal will quickly back down.

00:19:25.340 --> 00:19:28.700
These two are equally matched, and they will fight.

00:19:47.220 --> 00:19:55.550
But even whip spiders were not the most formidable hunters in these forests.

00:19:56.140 --> 00:19:59.400
There were others with an even more venomous weaponry.

00:20:05.500 --> 00:20:12.140
This centipede has powerful jaws, poison fangs, and is very, very fast.

00:20:12.690 --> 00:20:18.770
It's a very good hunter, but it's only half as long as my little finger.

00:20:20.040 --> 00:20:25.160
There are centipedes in the world, however, that are as big as my forearm.

00:20:27.340 --> 00:20:29.830
This is one of these alarming giants.

00:20:30.030 --> 00:20:33.720
It's over 13 inches, 35 centimetres long

00:20:33.720 --> 00:20:36.860
and with the muscular strength of a small snake.

00:20:37.700 --> 00:20:41.220
And the poison in its black-tipped fangs is lethal.

00:20:44.210 --> 00:20:47.410
It hunts in the dark, bat-haunted caves of Venezuela.

00:20:55.530 --> 00:20:57.510
The whip spider and the velvet worm,

00:20:57.670 --> 00:20:59.980
it uses its antennae to feed...

00:21:00.000 --> 00:21:06.840
for its victims. The beetles that swarm on the rocky floor of the cave are of no interest to it.

00:21:06.980 --> 00:21:22.360
It's after bigger prey and it knows it can find that by climbing. Its many legs give it a secure

00:21:22.360 --> 00:21:25.690
hold on the vertical rocks. It's heading for the ceiling.

00:21:32.180 --> 00:21:42.080
Now in the darkness, it can sense bats flying past it.

00:21:46.410 --> 00:21:50.110
Holding on with its hind legs, it reaches out into their flight paths

00:21:50.110 --> 00:21:53.420
and almost immediately, it has one.

00:22:00.000 --> 00:22:04.460
An injection of venom from its fangs kills the bat almost instantaneously.

00:22:08.800 --> 00:22:12.910
It will take it an hour or so, but it will eat all the bat's flesh.

00:22:21.310 --> 00:22:23.750
So all these animals, having left the sea,

00:22:24.150 --> 00:22:28.880
solved the problems of moving around and breathing air in their own differing ways.

00:22:29.440 --> 00:22:29.980
But there was...

00:22:30.000 --> 00:22:36.400
another difficulty, mating. In the sea, animals need only release their eggs and sperm and the

00:22:36.400 --> 00:22:42.720
water mix the two together. On dry land, that couldn't happen, even for the most moisture-loving

00:22:42.720 --> 00:22:51.260
of creatures. An individual slug carries both male and female organs, but even then that was

00:22:51.260 --> 00:22:59.650
of no help. Each had to both give and receive. Somehow or other, pairs of individuals had to get together.

00:23:00.570 --> 00:23:05.560
And the ways they have evolved in which to do so, quite extraordinary.

00:23:06.190 --> 00:23:09.720
Indeed, some of them are almost beyond imagining.

00:23:11.710 --> 00:23:15.520
The leopard slug, you might think, has the simplest of habits.

00:23:16.010 --> 00:23:19.260
Maybe, but not when it comes to mating.

00:23:20.990 --> 00:23:23.030
When an individual is looking for a partner,

00:23:23.270 --> 00:23:27.490
it gives its trail of slime a special taste that advertises the fact.

00:23:28.880 --> 00:23:29.980
Another, if it feels...

00:23:30.000 --> 00:23:38.200
the same way, will detect the invitation and start to follow. The pursuer, to confirm that it's there

00:23:38.200 --> 00:23:51.090
and ready to mate, gives the pursued a nibble. The leader heads upwards. An overhang is what's needed.

00:24:05.050 --> 00:24:08.130
the underside of a branch will do very nicely.

00:24:08.830 --> 00:24:11.710
The two start to circle one another more and more closely

00:24:11.710 --> 00:24:13.960
until they entwine.

00:24:18.180 --> 00:24:21.700
For an hour or so, they continue to wind themselves around one another.

00:24:32.170 --> 00:24:38.440
Then, suddenly, the pair release their hold on the branch

00:24:38.440 --> 00:24:42.270
and start to slide downwards on a rope of mucus.

00:25:00.030 --> 00:25:03.460
In mid-air, they move to the next stage in their pairing.

00:25:05.010 --> 00:25:08.810
Each everts its male organ from just behind its head.

00:25:19.210 --> 00:25:21.500
These grow longer and longer.

00:25:30.380 --> 00:25:33.590
Then they, too, begin to entwine.

00:25:56.540 --> 00:25:59.900
They fan out to form a translucent...

00:26:00.060 --> 00:26:20.510
flower-like glow. Now, at last, sperm passes from one slug to the other. Transfer is complete.

00:26:20.940 --> 00:26:22.440
Each has been fertilized.

00:26:32.680 --> 00:26:40.000
Finally, their strange, poetic relationship comes to an end with a bump.

00:26:43.560 --> 00:26:47.560
A millipede, unlike a slug, is either a male or a female.

00:26:48.890 --> 00:26:51.630
In southern Africa, where there are many different species,

00:26:52.240 --> 00:26:54.600
both sexes spend the winter in hibernation,

00:26:55.100 --> 00:26:57.520
curled up in the leaf litter or beneath the bark.

00:27:00.000 --> 00:27:01.820
Temperatures rise with the coming of spring.

00:27:02.040 --> 00:27:05.220
They all unwind themselves and set off to look for a mate.

00:27:06.400 --> 00:27:09.520
Finding one in the tangled undergrowth is not easy.

00:27:11.300 --> 00:27:17.270
But this male forest millipede knows that he can increase his chances

00:27:17.270 --> 00:27:21.080
if he heads upwards into the trees.

00:27:26.630 --> 00:27:29.770
Leaving the safety of the undergrowth may seem a risky thing to do.

00:27:30.160 --> 00:27:33.500
But these millipedes secrete a poison from paws in their armour,

00:27:33.720 --> 00:27:38.100
and their conspicuous red and black colours warn predators to leave them alone.

00:27:38.100 --> 00:27:40.800
They emerge in thousands.

00:27:45.810 --> 00:27:49.130
Surprisingly, perhaps, a male, when he does find a female,

00:27:49.350 --> 00:27:51.430
is not met with a friendly greeting.

00:27:51.630 --> 00:27:52.570
Quite the reverse.

00:27:52.790 --> 00:27:53.890
She coils up.

00:27:59.080 --> 00:27:59.960
This is her...

00:28:00.000 --> 00:28:05.860
way of sorting out the men from the boys. Only the strongest and fittest male will have the strength

00:28:05.860 --> 00:28:13.690
to force her coils apart. To help him do so he has white suction pads on the bottom of his feet

00:28:13.690 --> 00:28:22.490
which give him a good grip. Eventually she relaxes and he lifts her up so he can extend

00:28:22.490 --> 00:28:26.370
two specially modified legs with which he inseminates her.

00:28:32.040 --> 00:28:37.130
Once inserted, these legs swell so that the partners become fastened together.

00:28:37.130 --> 00:28:42.710
And that's important, because it will take him a couple of hours to transfer his sperm.

00:28:45.560 --> 00:28:50.400
But there are lots of males around, and before long, another one turns up.

00:28:54.740 --> 00:28:57.740
The new arrival checks out the pair with his antennae.

00:28:58.070 --> 00:28:59.970
If they're not tightly bound together...

00:29:00.120 --> 00:29:02.080
he may have a chance of taking over.

00:29:06.690 --> 00:29:09.110
He pushes between them, levering them apart.

00:29:10.800 --> 00:29:13.880
Gradually, he manages to unzip their legs.

00:29:18.020 --> 00:29:21.040
The first male's white mating legs are dragged out.

00:29:22.620 --> 00:29:23.600
He's been defeated.

00:29:24.170 --> 00:29:28.020
It will be the second, stronger male who fertilises her eggs.

00:29:32.040 --> 00:29:36.700
So mating on land isn't as random as it had been for so many in the sea.

00:29:36.940 --> 00:29:42.400
Now it's selective, but brute force isn't the only basis on which to select.

00:29:43.900 --> 00:29:46.480
Her female springtail is bigger than a male,

00:29:46.500 --> 00:29:51.470
and she prefers a partner who can give her a sustained head-to-head push.

00:29:54.450 --> 00:29:56.470
Other males are eager to try their luck,

00:29:57.000 --> 00:29:59.540
but butting her sides won't get them anywhere.

00:30:14.330 --> 00:30:16.670
She seems unimpressed by any of them,

00:30:17.150 --> 00:30:20.050
but one is determined to stay as her dance partner.

00:30:33.420 --> 00:30:35.240
She simply can't get rid of him.

00:30:42.120 --> 00:30:46.820
He confidently signals victory with a couple of fancy twirls.

00:30:49.440 --> 00:30:52.540
Then he deposits a droplet of sperm onto the leaf,

00:30:52.660 --> 00:30:55.700
and she graciously takes it on board.

00:31:01.400 --> 00:31:09.100
One group of colonists were of particular importance,

00:31:09.100 --> 00:31:11.630
for they changed the nature of the soil

00:31:12.010 --> 00:31:16.130
and thus made it possible for new kinds of plants and animals to evolve.

00:31:17.310 --> 00:31:20.990
They outweigh all other animals in any given area of the forest.

00:31:21.470 --> 00:31:24.690
A single hectare may be home to eight million of them.

00:31:26.800 --> 00:31:29.630
They spend nearly all their time below ground.

00:31:31.980 --> 00:31:39.820
They eat their way through the earth, extracting edible vegetable material and making it suitable for plants.

00:31:42.350 --> 00:31:46.270
At night, they come to the surface and collect dead leaves.

00:32:00.000 --> 00:32:04.880
also take the opportunity to call on their neighbours, poking their heads into next-door

00:32:04.880 --> 00:32:14.150
burrows. They're looking for partners. Like slugs, they're hermaphrodite, each individual

00:32:14.150 --> 00:32:22.970
both male and female. They mate by lying alongside one another. Two narrow grooves form between

00:32:22.970 --> 00:32:28.350
their two bodies. These are the conduits that carry sperm from one partner to the other.

00:32:31.850 --> 00:32:36.350
Their bodies slowly pulse as sperm travels along the space between them.

00:32:36.900 --> 00:32:42.030
But the process is a long one, and it may be three hours or so before they separate,

00:32:42.370 --> 00:32:44.110
each carrying the other's sperm.

00:32:51.740 --> 00:32:54.280
Like so many of the inhabitants of the undergrowth,

00:32:54.790 --> 00:32:57.170
earthworms can only live in a moist environment,

00:32:57.600 --> 00:32:59.960
but they are found in soils of every...

00:33:00.000 --> 00:33:07.870
continent except Antarctica. This small valley in southern Australia is home to one of the rarest

00:33:07.870 --> 00:33:15.690
and the most extraordinary of all earthworms. And I know they're around because I can hear them.

00:33:20.650 --> 00:33:25.770
Those gurgling noises, believe it or not, are being made by giant earthworms

00:33:25.770 --> 00:33:28.610
as they squelch along their water-filled burrows.

00:33:36.490 --> 00:33:40.720
The vibrations of my footsteps are enough to stir them into activity.

00:33:44.920 --> 00:33:46.260
They never come to the surface.

00:33:46.800 --> 00:33:51.440
But in places where there's been a small landslip, you can sometimes find their burrows.

00:33:59.210 --> 00:33:59.970
They are over...

00:34:00.000 --> 00:34:05.930
an inch in diameter. And in them, if you're very lucky, you may occasionally find one

00:34:05.930 --> 00:34:16.520
of these. This is one of their egg cocoons. And it's enormous. If I hold it up against

00:34:16.520 --> 00:34:24.480
the light, I can see the young worm inside wriggling. It'll take a year for this to develop.

00:34:24.700 --> 00:34:29.450
And when the young one finally does break three, it's all ready.

00:34:30.000 --> 00:34:33.000
20 centimetres long, huge.

00:34:35.670 --> 00:34:39.270
It will take a further five years to reach full size

00:34:39.270 --> 00:34:41.790
and become this remarkable creature.

00:34:42.970 --> 00:34:47.550
So the question is, how long is a giant earthworm?

00:34:47.770 --> 00:34:50.020
Well, it's not an easy question to answer.

00:34:50.440 --> 00:34:55.110
The fact of the matter is they're rather delicate creatures and they break.

00:34:55.980 --> 00:34:59.640
If I was so unfeeling, I'd have to try and stretch it.

00:35:00.410 --> 00:35:06.010
Well, I guess it might stretch to a couple of metres, almost six feet long.

00:35:09.960 --> 00:35:10.780
How long they live?

00:35:11.210 --> 00:35:14.630
Well, some say up to 20 years, but we really don't know.

00:35:15.440 --> 00:35:19.660
And we certainly don't know how they manage to mate deep underground

00:35:19.660 --> 00:35:22.540
as they squelch their way through their lonely tunnels.

00:35:28.320 --> 00:35:29.980
The land may have been a safe place.

00:35:30.000 --> 00:35:35.660
for eggs when horseshoe crabs first laid theirs up on the beach, but as new kinds

00:35:35.660 --> 00:35:39.740
of animals appeared, so it became increasingly important for animals to

00:35:39.740 --> 00:35:45.690
protect their eggs. Most creatures just hid them, but a few now actively defend

00:35:45.690 --> 00:35:51.710
them. The builder of this circular mud wall in the Central American rainforest

00:35:51.710 --> 00:35:57.910
is one. During the day, he conceals himself, but when night comes, he emerges

00:35:57.910 --> 00:35:59.550
to inspect his collection.

00:36:00.000 --> 00:36:29.790
He's a relative of eggs. His body is smaller than a grain of wheat. He's a relative of the spiders, a harvestman. His eggs, up to 100 of them, are half buried in the floor of his nest, and he regularly inspects each one of them. If it has a fungus on it, he carefully...

00:36:30.000 --> 00:36:33.160
cleans it before putting it back into its moist bed.

00:36:33.680 --> 00:36:40.820
He also continually repairs and improves his nest,

00:36:41.190 --> 00:36:46.230
for females will only call on those who have well-built and well-kept ones.

00:36:49.020 --> 00:36:51.940
Some males, however, follow a different policy.

00:36:52.660 --> 00:36:54.780
They don't bother to build a nest for themselves.

00:36:55.000 --> 00:36:58.340
They try to take over an existing one.

00:37:00.000 --> 00:37:05.530
A nest holder has to leave sometimes to feed, and that gives an intruder his chance.

00:37:14.450 --> 00:37:23.540
Owner is back almost immediately, and they fight, trying to bite one another in the weakest point of their armor, the joints of the legs.

00:37:41.390 --> 00:37:45.740
The intruder retreats and the nest owner checks his eggs.

00:37:51.800 --> 00:37:52.480
Damage done.

00:37:57.900 --> 00:37:59.810
And then another more welcome.

00:38:00.000 --> 00:38:12.600
visitor arrives. This is a female. She's bigger than he is, and she's touring all the nests in

00:38:12.600 --> 00:38:23.850
the neighborhood to choose the one where her eggs will be best cared for. She seems to approve of

00:38:23.850 --> 00:38:29.980
the standard of his housekeeping. So now, face to face through the tangle of legs.

00:38:30.410 --> 00:38:31.350
She mates with him.

00:38:32.030 --> 00:38:38.100
He has a rod with which he injects his sperm.

00:38:45.240 --> 00:38:48.260
He withdraws, and she's been fertilized.

00:38:55.860 --> 00:38:59.820
Half an hour later, she lowers her white tubular ovipositor.

00:39:00.120 --> 00:39:02.220
feeling for a suitable place for her egg.

00:39:05.770 --> 00:39:08.210
She thrusts the egg into the floor of the nest

00:39:08.210 --> 00:39:10.910
and then covers it with a thin blanket of mud.

00:39:30.190 --> 00:39:35.280
She leaves. He will now tend and guard the egg with the rest of his collection

00:39:35.280 --> 00:39:37.680
for the month that it will take to hatch.

00:39:41.400 --> 00:39:44.220
His nest is clearly one of the best in the neighbourhood,

00:39:44.480 --> 00:39:47.940
for throughout the night, a succession of females call on him.

00:39:50.130 --> 00:39:54.250
But not all have come to lay. Life is not that simple.

00:39:56.500 --> 00:39:59.840
This one starts, as usual, with a routine inspection.

00:40:00.000 --> 00:40:03.040
and then, without more ado, she mates.

00:40:07.670 --> 00:40:12.200
He waits for her to produce a new egg, but nothing appears.

00:40:20.770 --> 00:40:23.630
Constantly checks the nest floor with his feelers,

00:40:24.160 --> 00:40:26.120
but there are no signs of a new egg.

00:40:31.760 --> 00:40:34.890
And then she grabs one from his collection.

00:40:35.090 --> 00:40:36.210
She wants to eat one.

00:40:39.280 --> 00:40:40.120
She grabs again.

00:40:41.600 --> 00:40:44.240
He bites her leg joints and tries to pull her away.

00:40:50.900 --> 00:40:51.620
She's had enough.

00:40:52.170 --> 00:40:54.070
And he has rescued his egg.

00:40:58.890 --> 00:40:59.650
Checks it over.

00:41:00.000 --> 00:41:03.950
cleans it with great care, and then takes it away to rebury it.

00:41:09.170 --> 00:41:13.180
A month after the eggs were laid, his young begin to emerge.

00:41:28.540 --> 00:41:29.980
Kins from which they hatched.

00:41:30.320 --> 00:41:32.000
provide them with their first meal.

00:41:34.060 --> 00:41:36.580
He will now guard his young for a couple of days

00:41:36.580 --> 00:41:38.040
until they leave the nest.

00:41:42.040 --> 00:41:45.260
Excellently adapted though harvestmen are to a life on land,

00:41:45.460 --> 00:41:49.580
they cannot survive for very long away from this damp undergrowth.

00:41:53.040 --> 00:41:55.630
In fact, most of the direct descendants

00:41:55.630 --> 00:41:58.150
of those early colonists that came from the sea

00:41:58.150 --> 00:41:59.970
are still trapped in a world of land.

00:42:00.410 --> 00:42:15.520
Those with no external skeletons are always in imminent danger of death by drought.

00:42:20.670 --> 00:42:23.570
Even those with exoskeletons are not safe,

00:42:23.950 --> 00:42:29.510
for most have armour that is not totally watertight and will eventually dry out and die.

00:42:30.030 --> 00:42:32.710
if they leave the dank shelter of the undergrowth.

00:42:42.440 --> 00:42:44.640
But beyond the reach of the forests,

00:42:45.020 --> 00:42:48.140
in the centre of continents where little or no rain falls,

00:42:48.580 --> 00:42:52.740
there is a very different territory, empty and hostile.

00:42:59.620 --> 00:42:59.980
Here...

00:43:00.190 --> 00:43:02.830
There is little shelter from the scorching sun.

00:43:03.700 --> 00:43:06.760
Temperatures rise above 70 degrees centigrade

00:43:06.760 --> 00:43:10.900
and there may be no rain whatsoever for years on end.

00:43:19.940 --> 00:43:23.910
Deserts like this one in the southwest of the United States

00:43:23.910 --> 00:43:27.980
represented the ultimate challenge for those ancient creatures

00:43:27.980 --> 00:43:29.980
whose ancestors first...

00:43:30.320 --> 00:43:39.370
left the sea. Here there's virtually no water at all and yet those early creatures, the very first

00:43:39.370 --> 00:43:49.760
to walk on land, reached even here and they're still around. In order to survive the ferocious

00:43:49.760 --> 00:43:56.690
heat of the day they take refuge in little burrows like this which go quite a long way

00:43:56.910 --> 00:43:58.050
down into the ground.

00:43:58.950 --> 00:43:59.970
But I can use

00:44:00.000 --> 00:44:04.930
Here's this special optical probe to see whether anyone's at home.

00:44:21.520 --> 00:44:24.500
And there it is, it's a scorpion.

00:44:25.710 --> 00:44:29.180
They won't come out for the rest of the day, but at night.

00:44:30.000 --> 00:44:34.200
When it gets cool, scorpions all over the desert will be emerging.

00:44:35.750 --> 00:44:39.470
And then we have a very special way of finding them.

00:44:42.460 --> 00:44:46.160
In ultraviolet light, scorpions are magically transformed.

00:44:46.480 --> 00:44:48.500
They glow with fluorescence.

00:44:49.960 --> 00:44:53.800
So, with an ultraviolet torch, you can get a better idea

00:44:53.800 --> 00:44:58.580
of just how abundant scorpions actually are, even in this arid wilderness.

00:45:02.840 --> 00:45:08.760
That's because they have managed to develop external skeletons that are virtually watertight.

00:45:11.500 --> 00:45:16.780
They also have powerful stings and pincers, so getting together to mate could be dangerous.

00:45:17.960 --> 00:45:22.470
A male looking for a female must be careful.

00:45:26.320 --> 00:45:29.380
She is powerful enough to kill and eat him.

00:45:33.930 --> 00:45:36.460
So he begins to dance.

00:45:37.100 --> 00:45:45.710
Is she impressed?

00:45:56.830 --> 00:45:57.610
Apparently so.

00:45:58.280 --> 00:45:59.720
And his solo becomes...

00:46:00.000 --> 00:46:18.140
a pas de deux, but stings

00:46:18.140 --> 00:46:19.720
are still held high,

00:46:19.900 --> 00:46:21.040
ready to strike.

00:46:43.080 --> 00:46:44.260
Tries to sting him.

00:46:49.130 --> 00:46:53.390
His response is to give her a dose of her own medicine with a quick jab.

00:46:58.800 --> 00:46:59.780
But it's so slight.

00:47:00.090 --> 00:47:01.990
It merely makes her a little drowsy.

00:47:03.180 --> 00:47:07.700
At last, she seems more amenable.

00:47:08.230 --> 00:47:11.730
He pulls her to a part of the dance ground that is smooth and level.

00:47:12.360 --> 00:47:16.740
He has extruded a small packet of sperm on a stalk glued to the ground.

00:47:18.530 --> 00:47:20.850
He maneuvers her so that as she dances,

00:47:20.870 --> 00:47:24.930
she goes over the stalk and takes a sperm packet up into her body.

00:47:30.120 --> 00:47:40.580
dance is over. Her fertilized eggs stay within a special chamber in her body for more than a year

00:47:40.800 --> 00:47:52.770
while they slowly develop and then in her burrow deep underground she gives birth. She has produced

00:47:52.770 --> 00:47:57.810
up to 50 young ones. They cling tightly to her back for a few weeks after birth,

00:47:58.030 --> 00:47:59.970
each sustained by a small blob.

00:48:00.000 --> 00:48:09.870
of yolk in its stomach and then at last they're all ready to venture into the open desert for

00:48:09.990 --> 00:48:23.120
themselves by colonizing this the most hostile of environments the first animals to walk on land

00:48:23.120 --> 00:48:29.980
finally broke their link with open water and they did that about 300 million years

00:48:30.000 --> 00:48:38.250
At a time when the animals with backbones, including our own ancestors, were still swimming in the seas.
